Angular creates different instances at each provider boundary. Some consumers receive the root instance, route descendants may receive the route instance, and the child subtree receives the component instance. Each instance starts its own state and possibly its own socket, so the application no longer has one source of truth.

First map every provider registration and log instance creation with a temporary instance ID. Decide who owns the state:
- application session state belongs at root;
- feature state that should reset on route exit belongs at the route;
- isolated reusable-widget state belongs at the component.

Register the service at only the chosen boundary. Split long-lived infrastructure such as a shared socket transport from feature-level state if their lifetimes differ. Ensure teardown occurs when route/component providers are destroyed. Add tests asserting instance identity, number of socket connections, and state reset behavior across navigation.

