A refresh-token solution needs a single-flight coordinator. When several requests receive 401 at the same time, only one refresh call should run; eligible failed requests wait for that result and then retry with the new credential.

Important boundaries:
- never apply refresh handling recursively to the refresh endpoint;
- distinguish 401 authentication failure from 403 authorization failure;
- cap retry to one refresh attempt per original request;
- rebuild the retried request with the new token;
- on refresh failure, clear session state once and release waiting requests;
- do not automatically replay a non-idempotent command unless the API's idempotency design makes it safe.

Authentication storage and CSRF design depend on the architecture. Secure HttpOnly/SameSite cookies reduce JavaScript token exposure but require appropriate CSRF controls. Browser-stored bearer tokens require strict XSS risk management and short lifetimes.

