A real-time dashboard can be slow at several layers: too many network messages, expensive normalization, broad state invalidation, repeated calculations, too many DOM nodes, chart redraws, layout/paint, memory pressure, or long main-thread tasks.

In Angular 22, OnPush and zoneless behavior are already defaults, so “turn on OnPush” and “remove ZoneJS” are not complete answers. The main work is controlling how much data reaches the browser, how often UI state changes, and how many consumers are invalidated.

A useful target architecture is:
1. ingest messages once;
2. normalize and batch them;
3. update only affected entities;
4. expose narrow computed selectors per widget;
5. render at a human-perceivable cadence;
6. virtualize or defer off-screen content;
7. isolate and destroy expensive third-party visualizations.

